### diagnostics_channel: Patterns for Library Authors and APM Integration

Diagnostic channels are almost free when nobody listens, which is what makes them safe for an APM agent (application performance monitoring) to use in production. For async operations the pattern is one channel per lifecycle event: start, end and error.

Well-designed channels also follow a naming convention, `<package>:<component>:<event>` — for example `undici:request:create` or `mongodb:commandStarted`. That convention is what enables targeted subscriptions and wildcard-based APM instrumentation.

Node.js 18+ exposes built-in channels for the HTTP client (undici), which `fetch` uses internally, and for `net`. Subscribing to those channels gives you HTTP tracing with zero modifications to your own code.

```typescript
import { channel, subscribe, unsubscribe, Channel } from 'diagnostics_channel';

// Pattern: library publishes to multiple lifecycle channels
const channels = {
  start: channel('mylib:http:request-start'),
  end:   channel('mylib:http:request-end'),
  error: channel('mylib:http:request-error'),
};

async function httpRequest(url: string, options: RequestInit) {
  const requestData = { url, method: options.method ?? 'GET', startTime: Date.now() };

  if (channels.start.hasSubscribers) channels.start.publish(requestData);
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url, options);
    if (channels.end.hasSubscribers) {
      channels.end.publish({
        ...requestData,
        statusCode: response.status,
        duration: Date.now() - requestData.startTime,
      });
    }
    return response;
  } catch (err) {
    if (channels.error.hasSubscribers) {
      channels.error.publish({
        ...requestData,
        error: err,
        duration: Date.now() - requestData.startTime,
      });
    }
    throw err;
  }
}

// APM subscriber — collect all HTTP metrics
subscribe('mylib:http:request-end', ({ url, statusCode, duration }) => {
  metrics.histogram('http.client.duration', duration, { url, statusCode });
});

// Unsubscribe cleanup (important in tests)
const handler = (data) => console.log(data);
subscribe('mylib:http:request-start', handler);
// later:
unsubscribe('mylib:http:request-start', handler);
```

In the example the library owns three channels and publishes to each one only when it has subscribers. The start event carries the URL and the method. The end event adds the status code and the duration. The error event adds the thrown error. The APM subscriber at the bottom needs only the end channel to build a latency histogram.

The `unsubscribe` call matters in tests: a leftover subscriber would keep collecting events from the next test.

**Common interview trap:** using one channel for every event and putting the event type in the payload. Every subscriber then has to filter, which burns processor (CPU) time on events it does not care about. One channel per event type means a subscriber pays only for what it needs.

One detail to plan for: `subscribe()` returns nothing. To unsubscribe you have to pass the very same function reference, so a test does need to keep it. The `unsubscribe()` call returns `true` when the handler was found and removed.

